      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-342288",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$\\frac{dy}{dt} + y = \\sin t - \\cos t, y(0) = 1$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$L{\\sin \u03c9t} = \\frac{\u03c9}{s^{2} + \u03c9^{2}}, L{\\cos \u03c9t} = \\frac{s}{s^{2} + \u03c9^{2}}, s > 0$.\n\nLet $Y(s) = L{y(t)}$. Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y$, we have\n\n$(s^{2} + 2)/((s + 1)(s^{2} + 1))$ {{field-1}}"
